      "nets": ["5V_CAN_ENABLE / SER_SELECT / EXTRA_GPIO (PCA9536D side)", "USBAP_D_P / USBAP_D_N / 5V_FUSED (USBLC6-2P6 side)"],
      "observed": "The reference designator U26 is assigned to two different components on two different sheets: on the I/O sheet it labels the PCA9536D I2C GPIO expander (pins 100-103 driving 5V_CAN_ENABLE, SER_SELECT, EXTRA_GPIO), and on the USB sheet it labels one of the USBLC6-2P6 USB ESD/TVS arrays (the companion array being U25).",
      "expected": "Every component must carry a unique reference so the BOM and placement netlist resolve to two distinct parts; the PCA9536D and the USBLC6-2P6 are unrelated devices and cannot share a designator.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "I/O sheet: symbol box 'U26' with value 'PCA9536D', pins 100=5V_CAN_ENABLE, 102=SER_SELECT, 103=EXTRA_GPIO"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "USB sheet: ESD array symbol 'U26' with value 'USBLC6-2P6' on USBAP_D_P/USBAP_D_N/5V_FUSED, next to the 'U25' USBLC6-2P6 array"}
      ],
      "impact": "A duplicated reference is a design/assembly error: during BOM generation and pick-and-place the two parts collide, so one of them (either the I2C expander that drives 5V_CAN_ENABLE/SER_SELECT/EXTRA_GPIO, or the USB ESD protection on the USB-A port) will be omitted or populated with the wrong part, breaking that function.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The CAN crystals (Y1/Y2) are correctly 16 MHz, the e-stop sink buffers and indicator chain match the spec, and the USB ideal-diode path is a documented open issue; however the drawing assigns reference U26 to two unrelated components (the PCA9536D I2C expander and a USBLC6-2P6 USB ESD array), which is a design/assembly anomaly that would cause one of those parts to be mis-sourced or omitted."
}

